1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,834
In Madrid’s Prado Museum, one painting makes sorrow feel as if it has been carved into the bodies themselves.

2
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:12,400
Christ has just been lowered from the cross.

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:22,612
At his feet, the Virgin Mary collapses in grief, her body bending backward in a curve that echoes the falling body of her son.

4
00:00:22,612 --> 00:00:30,150
They do not embrace, yet they repeat the same sorrowful gesture like two echoes of one wound.

5
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:35,094
This is Rogier van der Weyden’s *The Descent from the Cross*.

6
00:00:35,094 --> 00:00:40,362
He left no reliable autobiography and very few secure signatures.

7
00:00:40,362 --> 00:00:52,276
Yet he made sorrow exist not only in tears and faces, but in shoulders, fingers, sleeves, and the direction in which one body leans toward another.

8
00:00:52,276 --> 00:01:05,000
If Jan van Eyck makes us see how light rests on metal, fur, and mirrors, van der Weyden makes us feel how emotion passes through the body and enters our own.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,176
Rogier van der Weyden was probably born in Tournai around 1399 or 1400.

10
00:01:10,176 --> 00:01:13,603
His early French name was Rogier de la Pasture.

11
00:01:13,603 --> 00:01:21,841
After he moved into the Dutch-speaking environment of Brussels, the name was translated as Rogier van der Weyden.

12
00:01:21,841 --> 00:01:28,256
These are not two artists, but one person moving through different languages and cities.

13
00:01:28,256 --> 00:01:33,943
The fifteenth-century Netherlands did not have today’s clear national borders.

14
00:01:33,943 --> 00:01:41,015
French, Dutch, Latin, and local dialects overlapped in courts, churches, workshops, and commerce.

15
00:01:41,015 --> 00:01:45,097
Van der Weyden’s career developed inside those networks.

16
00:01:45,097 --> 00:01:52,242
His father, Henri de le Pasture, was a master cutler, placing the family in the urban craft class.

17
00:01:52,242 --> 00:01:56,981
The record of Rogier’s childhood and earliest training is sparse.

18
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:05,000
We should not romanticize him as a destitute genius, nor invent a fully documented family dynasty of painters.

19
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,944
In 1427, a Tournai guild document records a man named Rogelet de la Pasture entering the workshop of Robert Campin.

20
00:02:12,944 --> 00:02:17,710
On August 1, 1432, he became a master in the Tournai painters’ guild.

21
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:25,723
Scholars still debate how exactly this Rogelet relates to the later van der Weyden and how long his training lasted.

22
00:02:25,723 --> 00:02:34,772
Most accept that the two figures were probably the same, but the details of his early formation cannot be reconstructed completely.

23
00:02:34,772 --> 00:02:46,031
The Campin workshop brought sacred stories into recognizable rooms: benches, fireplaces, water jugs, lilies, and wooden windows became the setting for holy events.

24
00:02:46,031 --> 00:02:56,461
Van der Weyden inherited close observation of cloth, wood, metal, and faces, as well as the ability to organize private devotion through an altarpiece.

25
00:02:56,461 --> 00:02:59,293
But he moved toward a different emphasis.

26
00:02:59,293 --> 00:03:03,714
He compressed spatial depth and pushed bodies toward the viewer.

27
00:03:03,714 --> 00:03:10,000
The environment no longer carried the main narrative.Bodies, folds, and lines of sight did.

28
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,727
Around 1435, van der Weyden moved to Brussels.

29
00:03:13,727 --> 00:03:18,669
From March 2, 1436, he served as the city’s official painter.

30
00:03:18,669 --> 00:03:34,468
Brussels was the political and cultural center of the Burgundian Netherlands, where the duke’s court, nobles, officials, and foreign envoys generated commissions for paintings and luxury objects.

31
00:03:34,468 --> 00:03:44,028
For the town hall’s Golden Chamber, he painted four monumental panels known as *The Justice of Trajan and Herkinbald*.

32
00:03:44,028 --> 00:03:47,593
Their subject was rulers dispensing justice.

33
00:03:47,593 --> 00:03:52,454
Albrecht Dürer reportedly made a special effort to see them.

34
00:03:52,454 --> 00:04:02,338
The panels were destroyed when Brussels was bombarded in 1695; tapestry copies and documentary traces are all that remain.

35
00:04:02,338 --> 00:04:10,116
This lost commission matters.Van der Weyden did not paint only Virgins and martyrs for churches.

36
00:04:10,116 --> 00:04:12,627
He also designed public memory.

37
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:20,000
His visual language of grief could serve justice, civic identity, and political legitimacy.

38
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:29,098
According to the Prado, *The Descent from the Cross* was painted around 1435–38 and completed no later than 1443.

39
00:04:29,098 --> 00:04:36,345
It is oil on oak, approximately 220 by 262 centimeters, and now hangs in the Prado Museum.

40
00:04:36,345 --> 00:04:44,477
The original commission came from the Leuven crossbowmen’s guild for a chapel outside the city walls.

41
00:04:44,477 --> 00:04:49,388
Two small crossbows in the tracery preserve the guild’s mark.

42
00:04:49,388 --> 00:04:55,185
The painting resembles a golden wooden box or a shallow sculpted shrine.

43
00:04:55,185 --> 00:04:59,452
Ten life-size figures have almost nowhere to retreat.

44
00:04:59,452 --> 00:05:04,847
Shoulders, arms, sleeves, and feet approach the edges of the frame.

45
00:05:04,847 --> 00:05:11,530
The central tragedy is not staged with storms, black clouds, or a bloody landscape.

46
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:18,213
It is carried by two bodies.Christ slides down from the cross while Mary collapses.

47
00:05:18,213 --> 00:05:26,989
Their bodies form related curves, allowing Mary to repeat the posture of Christ’s death through her own body.

48
00:05:26,989 --> 00:05:34,235
John supports Mary.Joseph and Nicodemus receive Christ.Mary Magdalene twists at the right.

49
00:05:34,235 --> 00:05:39,952
Everyone is trying to hold someone else, yet no gesture can undo death.

50
00:05:39,952 --> 00:05:47,762
Van der Weyden paints the tears small, but makes them catch the light through transparent glazes.

51
00:05:47,762 --> 00:05:54,203
Reddened skin, clenched fingers, and compressed fabric turn emotion into matter.

52
00:05:54,203 --> 00:06:00,000
The more rigorously the grief is ordered, the more inescapable it feels.

53
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,899
Around 1435–40, van der Weyden painted *Saint Luke Drawing the Virgin*.

54
00:06:04,899 --> 00:06:12,833
The Boston version is one of the most important surviving examples and is often considered closest to the original.

55
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:23,114
Related versions survive in Munich, Saint Petersburg, and Bruges; the degree of autograph work and workshop involvement remains a technical question.

56
00:06:23,114 --> 00:06:27,599
Luke kneels before the Virgin with a silverpoint or metal stylus.

57
00:06:27,599 --> 00:06:35,810
He resembles a court painter facing a sacred model, but he also becomes an allegory of van der Weyden’s own profession.

58
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:41,398
Some writers have suggested that Luke’s face contains a projection of the artist.

59
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:46,435
There is not enough evidence to identify him securely as a self-portrait.

60
00:06:46,435 --> 00:06:56,992
A safer reading is that van der Weyden places himself inside the theological tradition of Saint Luke, declaring that painting is not merely manual labor.

61
00:06:56,992 --> 00:06:59,683
It can be witness, prayer, and service.

62
00:06:59,683 --> 00:07:06,444
The interior, river, bridge, and distant city recall Jan van Eyck’s *Madonna of Chancellor Rolin*.

63
00:07:06,444 --> 00:07:11,895
But van der Weyden replaces the powerful donor with an artist kneeling at work.

64
00:07:11,895 --> 00:07:15,000
Optical marvel becomes professional humility.

65
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:23,698
The *Miraflores Altarpiece* was probably made in the first half of the 1440s, often placed around 1440–45.

66
00:07:23,698 --> 00:07:32,396
Its three panels show the Holy Family, Mary mourning Christ, and the risen Christ appearing to his mother.

67
00:07:32,396 --> 00:07:35,268
This is not a record of one moment.

68
00:07:35,268 --> 00:07:43,556
It compresses birth, death, and resurrection into a theater that a believer can read again and again.

69
00:07:43,556 --> 00:07:56,439
The *Seven Sacraments Altarpiece* distributes baptism, confirmation, confession, Eucharist, marriage, ordination, and extreme unction inside a Gothic church.

70
00:07:56,439 --> 00:08:01,854
A central crucifix becomes the theological source of every ritual.

71
00:08:01,854 --> 00:08:06,121
Past sacrifice and present liturgy occupy one space.

72
00:08:06,121 --> 00:08:14,819
The work may have been commissioned by Jean Chevrot, bishop of Tournai, but no complete contract survives.

73
00:08:14,819 --> 00:08:22,779
His portrait and the arms of Tournai provide important clues, not a substitute for documentation.

74
00:08:22,779 --> 00:08:30,000
Van der Weyden shows that an altarpiece can hold time the way architecture holds bodies.

75
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:44,647
Around 1443–51, perhaps as late as 1452, Nicolas Rolin and his wife commissioned the *Last Judgment Altarpiece* for the Hôtel-Dieu in Beaune.

76
00:08:44,647 --> 00:08:50,776
The work originally stood at the end of the patients’ ward.

77
00:08:50,776 --> 00:09:01,683
People lying in their beds could see Christ, Michael, the scales, the resurrected dead, Heaven, and Hell.

78
00:09:01,683 --> 00:09:08,643
Michael’s scales divide the space between salvation and punishment.

79
00:09:08,643 --> 00:09:15,291
Van der Weyden does not rely mainly on monsters to produce fear.

80
00:09:15,291 --> 00:09:24,328
He uses frightened faces, the weight of bodies, and directions that cannot be reversed.

81
00:09:24,328 --> 00:09:29,834
The donors remain visible within the religious scene.

82
00:09:29,834 --> 00:09:36,170
Their portraits and heraldry do not erase their public power.

83
00:09:36,170 --> 00:09:45,000
Charity, political reputation, and personal hope for salvation occupy the same image.

84
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:53,941
Around the Jubilee of 1450, van der Weyden traveled to Italy, probably including Rome and perhaps as a pilgrim.

85
00:09:53,941 --> 00:10:02,639
Courts and collectors in Ferrara and Florence encountered Netherlandish painting through works and exchange.

86
00:10:02,639 --> 00:10:11,741
The importance of this journey is not that it suddenly turned van der Weyden into an Italian Renaissance painter.

87
00:10:11,741 --> 00:10:17,782
It shows that fifteenth-century art did not develop along two sealed roads.

88
00:10:17,782 --> 00:10:24,628
Paintings, merchants, envoys, marriages, and pilgrims carried styles across the Alps.

89
00:10:24,628 --> 00:10:30,991
Italian viewers admired Northern material description, portraiture, and detail.

90
00:10:30,991 --> 00:10:36,710
Van der Weyden may have encountered Italian symmetry and monumentality.

91
00:10:36,710 --> 00:10:45,006
Yet even in his late works, he retained shallow stages, sharp folds, gold grounds, and intense emotion.

92
00:10:45,006 --> 00:10:50,000
Travel created exchange without erasing his Northern language.

93
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,575
Van der Weyden’s portraits often use the three-quarter view.

94
00:10:54,575 --> 00:11:01,972
Sitters stand against plain backgrounds with hands folded below.This is not photographic copying.

95
00:11:01,972 --> 00:11:09,749
He could lengthen a nose, narrow a face, or raise a forehead, balancing resemblance with social image.

96
00:11:09,749 --> 00:11:16,307
The portrait of Philip the Good survives largely through workshop versions and copies.

97
00:11:16,307 --> 00:11:25,915
Black clothing, the Order of the Golden Fleece, and a severe expression concentrate authority in the face, costume, and badge.

98
00:11:25,915 --> 00:11:38,954
In the portrait generally known as *Portrait of a Lady*, painted around 1460, a high forehead, transparent veil, lowered eyes, and folded hands construct modesty and rank.

99
00:11:38,954 --> 00:11:45,207
The high forehead also reflects the fashion of removing some hair at the hairline.

100
00:11:45,207 --> 00:11:49,782
These figures offer no narrative background to explain them.

101
00:11:49,782 --> 00:11:54,967
We must build their psychology from a mouth, a glance, and a finger.

102
00:11:54,967 --> 00:12:00,000
Silence becomes van der Weyden’s most efficient portrait language.

103
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,034
The *Braque Triptych*, painted around 1452, is now in the Louvre.

104
00:12:06,034 --> 00:12:17,454
The center and interior wings show Christ, the Virgin and Child, John the Baptist, Mary Magdalene, and John the Evangelist.

105
00:12:17,454 --> 00:12:22,560
When closed, the exterior presents a skull and a cross.

106
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:30,544
The exterior is not simply a gray decorative surface.It is a direct reminder of death.

107
00:12:30,544 --> 00:12:49,111
Louvre records connect the work with Jean Braque and Catherine de Brabant; it may have commemorated a deceased husband, but the precise commission and meanings of every symbol should remain qualified.

108
00:12:49,111 --> 00:12:55,424
The triptych could be opened, closed, and carried in a private room.

109
00:12:55,424 --> 00:12:59,231
The viewer had to open the wings by hand.

110
00:12:59,231 --> 00:13:10,000
Looking became a ritual: from death outside to salvation inside, from the memory of the dead to the image of prayer.

111
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:16,886
The *Columba Altarpiece* was probably made around 1450–55, often dated about 1455.

112
00:13:16,886 --> 00:13:32,674
Its central *Adoration of the Magi* joins the Virgin and Child to a distant Flemish city, showing that late van der Weyden could still balance powerful feeling with an expansive landscape.

113
00:13:32,674 --> 00:13:40,401
The *Crucifixion Diptych*, around 1460, strips the narrative down to Christ, Mary, and John.

114
00:13:40,401 --> 00:13:43,592
The background approaches abstraction.

115
00:13:43,592 --> 00:13:51,570
Emotion now depends on three bodies, the vertical of the cross, and a dark, nearly empty space.

116
00:13:51,570 --> 00:14:03,159
The work survives as a diptych, but technical research suggests that its panels may once have been the outer wings of a larger altarpiece.

117
00:14:03,159 --> 00:14:06,770
This uncertainty does not reduce its power.

118
00:14:06,770 --> 00:14:15,000
It reminds us that museum objects can be survivors of centuries of dismantling and reconstruction.

119
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:24,566
Van der Weyden rarely signed his works, while his workshop produced repeated compositions and portrait versions.

120
00:14:24,566 --> 00:14:33,108
Art historians therefore cannot decide authorship by asking only whether a picture “looks like” him.

121
00:14:33,108 --> 00:14:40,966
They combine wood-ring analysis, underdrawing, pigments, brushwork, and documentary history.

122
00:14:40,966 --> 00:14:45,834
Even a recognized masterpiece may include workshop labor.

123
00:14:45,834 --> 00:14:58,049
The master could design the composition and paint crucial faces and hands while assistants handled clothing, backgrounds, or repeated passages.

124
00:14:58,049 --> 00:15:04,113
This was not automatically deceptive.It was a normal production system.

125
00:15:04,113 --> 00:15:06,761
“Workshop” does not mean “fake.

126
00:15:06,761 --> 00:15:20,000
” Van der Weyden’s influence exists not only in one wooden panel, but in a language of emotion that studios, patrons, and European markets could reproduce.

127
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:29,704
Calling van der Weyden the “heart” and van Eyck the “eye” is useful for a first approach, but it cannot replace close looking.

128
00:15:29,704 --> 00:15:36,481
Both artists care about material, portraiture, religious symbolism, and exact structure.

129
00:15:36,481 --> 00:15:43,335
Van Eyck can express feeling, and van der Weyden possessed extraordinary technical skill.

130
00:15:43,335 --> 00:15:46,416
The difference is the center of gravity.

131
00:15:46,416 --> 00:15:55,735
Van Eyck often opens space toward the distance, allowing the eye to travel through mirrors, windows, cities, and objects.

132
00:15:55,735 --> 00:16:02,204
Van der Weyden often flattens space and pushes bodies forward like relief sculpture.

133
00:16:02,204 --> 00:16:07,903
The eye does not travel among objects; it follows arms, folds, and curves.

134
00:16:07,903 --> 00:16:12,601
*Saint Luke Drawing the Virgin* makes the connection visible.

135
00:16:12,601 --> 00:16:21,534
It borrows van Eyck’s interior, river, and city, but replaces a powerful chancellor with an artist kneeling at work.

136
00:16:21,534 --> 00:16:25,000
Optical marvel becomes professional humility.

137
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,805
Van der Weyden died in Brussels in 1464.

138
00:16:27,805 --> 00:16:37,972
Sources differ on the exact day and on details of his burial, but by the end of his life he was a public city figure, not an anonymous craftsman.

139
00:16:37,972 --> 00:16:48,069
His influence reached Hans Memling, Dieric Bouts, Hugo van der Goes, and Petrus Christus, and traveled into Germany, Spain, Portugal, and Italy.

140
00:16:48,069 --> 00:16:55,852
Later artists repeatedly borrowed his fainting Virgin, twisted arms, three-quarter portraits, and folded hands.

141
00:16:55,852 --> 00:17:15,135
When looking at van der Weyden, remember five things: follow parallel bodies; notice compressed space; watch tears, skin, and metal carry emotion into matter; see gold and skulls connect salvation to death; and read silent faces without forcing them to tell a complete story.

142
00:17:15,135 --> 00:17:21,656
He did not paint sorrow as one expression.He turned sorrow into a form the body can remember.

143
00:17:21,656 --> 00:17:23,830
This is **Our Famous Artists**.

144
00:17:23,830 --> 00:17:30,000
Thank you for watching, and join us next time as we step closer to another great artist.

